@Tutorial(time: 15) {
    @Intro(title: "Killing the player") {
        We can kill enemies by jumping on them, but the player still can't die.
        Let's fix this.

        We want to detect being hit by an enemy differently from squashing them.
        We want the player to die when they're moving on the floor, but not if
        they're in the air. We could use vector math to distinguish the two kinds
        of collisions. Instead, though, we will use an
        [Area3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Farea3d.html#class-area3d)
        node, which works well for hitboxes.
    }
    @Section(title: "Hitbox with the Area node") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We'll add an Area3D node to the `Player` that detects mob bodies. By
            tuning the size and position of the area, we make sure it only
            triggers when the player is on or near the ground.

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Head back to the `player.tscn` scene and add a new child node
                [Area3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Farea3d.html#class-area3d).
                Name it `MobDetector`. Add a
                [CollisionShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcollisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d)
                node as a child of it.

                @Image(source: 01.adding_area_node.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                In the _Inspector_, assign a cylinder shape to it.

                @Image(source: 02.cylinder_shape.webp)
            }
            Here is a trick you can use to make the collisions only happen when
            the player is on the ground or close to it. You can reduce the
            cylinder's height and move it up to the top of the character. This
            way, when the player jumps, the shape will be too high up for the
            enemies to collide with it.

            @Step {
                Reduce the cylinder's height and move it up to the top of the
                character.

                You also want the cylinder to be wider than the sphere. This
                way, the player gets hit before colliding and being pushed on
                top of the monster's collision box.

                > Tip: The wider the cylinder, the more easily the player
                > will get killed. This is a balance — too narrow and the
                > player can graze a monster without dying; too wide and
                > every encounter is fatal. Tune until it feels right.

                @Image(source: 03.cylinder_in_editor.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Select the `MobDetector` node again, and in the _Inspector_,
                turn **off** its _Monitorable_ property. This makes it so other
                physics nodes cannot detect the area. The complementary
                _Monitoring_ property allows it to detect collisions. Then,
                remove the _Collision -> Layer_ and set the mask to the
                "enemies" layer.

                @Image(source: 04.mob_detector_properties.webp)
            }
            When areas detect a collision, they emit signals. We're going to
            connect one to the `Player` node.

            @Step {
                Select `MobDetector` and go to the Signals dock, double-tap
                the `body_entered` signal and connect it to the `Player`.

                The _MobDetector_ will emit `body_entered` when a
                [CharacterBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcharacterbody3d.html#class-characterbody3d)
                or a
                [RigidBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Frigidbody3d.html#class-rigidbody3d)
                node enters it. As it only masks the "enemies" physics
                layers, it will only detect the `Mob` nodes.

                > Note: Unlike signals that come from `Control` nodes (like
                > a button), `Area3D` body signals are not automatically
                > shown in the editor's _Signals_ dock. We open the dock
                > manually from the _MobDetector_ node.

                @Image(source: 05.body_entered_signal.webp)
            }
            Code-wise, we're going to do two things: emit a signal we'll
            later use to end the game and destroy the player. We can wrap
            these operations in a `die()` function that helps us put a
            descriptive label on the code.

            @Step {
                Add the `hit` signal, the `die()` function, and the
                `_on_mob_detector_body_entered` callback to the player
                script.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-09.gd, previousFile: player-08.gd)
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Ending the game") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We can use the `Player`'s `hit` signal to end the game. All we
            need to do is connect it to the `Main` node and stop the
            `MobTimer` in reaction.

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Open `main.tscn`, select the `Player` node, and in the
                _Signals_ dock, connect its `hit` signal to the `Main` node.

                @Image(source: 06.player_hit_signal.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Get the timer, and stop it, in the `_on_player_hit()`
                function.

                @Code(name: "main.gd", file: main-03.gd, previousFile: main-02.gd)
            }
            If you try the game now, the monsters will stop spawning when
            you die, and the remaining ones will leave the screen.

            > Note: Notice also that the game no longer crashes or displays
            > an error when the player dies. Because we are stopping the
            > MobTimer, it no longer triggers the
            > `_on_mob_timer_timeout()` function — and the player node has
            > been queue_free'd, so any subsequent reference to `$Player`
            > in the timer callback would fail.

            Also note that the enemy colliding with the player and dying
            depends on the size and position of the `Player` and the `Mob`'s
            collision shapes. You may need to move them and resize them to
            achieve a tight game feel.

            You can pat yourself on the back: you prototyped a complete 3D
            game, even if it's still a bit rough.

            From there, we'll add a score, the option to retry the game,
            and you'll see how you can make the game feel much more alive
            with minimalistic animations.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Code checkpoint") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Here are the complete scripts for the `Main`, `Mob`, and `Player`
            nodes, for reference. You can use them to compare and check your
            code.

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Starting with `main.gd`.

                @Code(name: "main.gd", file: main-03.gd, reset: true)
            }
            @Step {
                Next is `mob.gd`.

                @Code(name: "mob.gd", file: mob-05.gd, reset: true)
            }
            @Step {
                Finally, the longest script, `player.gd`:

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-09.gd, reset: true)
            }
            See you in the next lesson to add the score and the retry
            option.
        }
    }
}
